WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.935
GPUs are acceleration hardware for local AI, inference servers, vision models, embeddings,

00:00:05.935 --> 00:00:08.200
development workflows, and data-heavy experimentation.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.060
A capable GPU can make model loading, prompt processing, image generation,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.770
vector search support, and batch inference feel practical.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:18.305
But the GPU only helps when the operating system, driver stack, runtime,

00:00:18.305 --> 00:00:20.720
and application can actually use it.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.840
Drivers sit between the Linux kernel, hardware, user-space libraries, containers,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.680
and AI applications.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.935
The kernel needs modules and firmware to communicate with the device.

00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:32.240
User-space libraries expose compute capabilities to runtimes.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.740
Containers need access to the host driver and device nodes.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.535
Python libraries, inference servers,

00:00:36.535 --> 00:00:39.360
and notebooks depend on that whole chain being compatible.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.840
There is a major difference between a detected GPU and a usable AI acceleration stack.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.051
A system may list a PCI device, but still fail to load the right driver,

00:00:47.051 --> 00:00:50.555
expose the wrong libraries, block access through permissions,

00:00:50.555 --> 00:00:52.640
or run the workload on the CPU by mistake.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.580
Good validation proves that the AI runtime can use acceleration,

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.680
not merely that the hardware exists.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.840
Driver stability affects performance, reliability, troubleshooting, and repeatability.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.175
An unmanaged update can break kernel modules.

00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:07.320
A random library mix can create confusing runtime errors.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.115
A system that worked yesterday may fail after a kernel upgrade.

00:01:10.115 --> 00:01:14.820
Treat GPU configuration as part of the AI system architecture, with planned versions,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.760
documentation, checks, and rollback options.
